  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code generation agents powered by large language models (LLMs) has revolutionized software development workflows. However, these powerful code agents also introduce significant security risks and vulnerabilities that must be addressed through systematic evaluation and red-teaming methodologies. This paper provides a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agents and red-teaming techniques. We examine the landscape of automated code generation systems, discuss the critical importance of security testing, and review state-of-the-art red-teaming frameworks including RedCodeAgent and BlueCodeAgent. We also analyze broader trustworthiness assessment frameworks from DecodingTrust and draw upon foundational neural network architectures from the Transformer and ResNet literature. Our work synthesizes recent research to provide a unified perspective on securing code agents through adversarial testing and blue teaming strategies.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents have emerged as transformative tools in the software engineering landscape, leveraging large language models to automate code generation, debugging, and optimization tasks. These agents, capable of generating complex software solutions from natural language descriptions, have found applications across diverse domains including web development, data science, and embedded systems. However, as highlighted by recent research, the integration of AI-powered code generation tools introduces novel attack surfaces and security vulnerabilities that require systematic evaluation [1].\n\nThe trustworthiness of code agents depends on multiple factors including code safety, security, correctness, and ethical compliance. Understanding and mitigating risks associated with these systems is paramount for their safe deployment in production environments. Red-teaming, the practice of adversarially testing systems to identify vulnerabilities, has become an essential methodology for evaluating the robustness of AI systems [2].\n\nIn this paper, we survey the state-of-the-art in code agents and red-teaming techniques. We examine the architectural foundations that enable modern code generation, review automated red-teaming frameworks specifically designed for code agents, and discuss emerging blue teaming strategies for defending against adversarial attacks. Our contributions include:\n\n1. A comprehensive overview of code agent architectures and capabilities\n2. Analysis of automated red-teaming frameworks for code generation systems\n3. Discussion of defense mechanisms and blue teaming approaches\n4. Examination of trustworthiness assessment methodologies for LLMs\n5. Synthesis of insights from foundational deep learning research\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Foundational Neural Network Architectures\n\nThe development of code agents builds upon several foundational neural network architectures that have revolutionized deep learning. The Transformer architecture, introduced by Vaswani et al., established the self-attention mechanism as a powerful alternative to recurrent and convolutional neural networks for sequence modeling tasks [3]. This architecture, which forms the backbone of most modern LLMs, enables parallel processing of sequences and has proven exceptionally effective for code generation tasks.\n\nResidual learning, as pioneered by He et al., addresses the degradation problem in deep neural networks by introducing skip connections that facilitate the training of substantially deeper networks [4]. While originally developed for image recognition, residual learning principles have influenced the design of deep architectures used in language understanding and generation, contributing to the development of larger and more capable code agents.\n\n### Trustworthiness Assessment of LLMs\n\nThe assessment of trustworthiness in LLMs has become a critical area of research. The DecodingTrust framework provides a comprehensive evaluation of various trustworthiness dimensions including harmlessness, robustness, bias, fairness, and factual consistency [2]. Their work demonstrates that while GPT models exhibit strong capabilities across various benchmarks, significant vulnerabilities remain that must be addressed through systematic evaluation and mitigation strategies.\n\n### Automated Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\nRecent work has focused on developing automated red-teaming frameworks specifically designed for code generation systems. RedCodeAgent represents a significant advancement in this area, providing an automatic red-teaming agent that can systematically test diverse code agents against various attack vectors [1]. Their framework identifies vulnerabilities in code generation systems by generating adversarial prompts and evaluating the generated code for security issues, code correctness, and safety violations.\n\nBuilding upon red-teaming insights, BlueCodeAgent introduces a complementary blue teaming approach that leverages automated red teaming to strengthen code generation systems [5]. This work demonstrates that systematic red-teaming can inform the development of more robust defense mechanisms, enabling code agents to better resist adversarial attacks and produce safer outputs.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agents and red-teaming is rapidly evolving, driven by both the increasing capabilities of LLMs and the growing recognition of security risks in AI-powered software development. Our survey has highlighted several key insights:\n\nFirst, the architectural foundations of modern code agents build upon seminal work in neural networks, particularly the Transformer architecture [3] and residual learning techniques [4], adapted for code generation tasks.\n\nSecond, trustworthiness assessment frameworks like DecodingTrust [2] provide essential methodologies for evaluating the safety and reliability of code agents across multiple dimensions.\n\nThird, automated red-teaming frameworks such as RedCodeAgent [1] and BlueCodeAgent [5] represent state-of-the-art approaches for systematically identifying and mitigating vulnerabilities in code generation systems.\n\nFuture work should focus on developing more sophisticated attack and defense mechanisms, establishing standardized benchmarks for code agent security, and creating adaptive red-teaming frameworks that can evolve with emerging threat landscapes. As code agents become increasingly integrated into software development pipelines, the continued advancement of red-teaming and defense methodologies will be essential for ensuring their safe and effective deployment.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv: 2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv: 2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[3] Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, et al. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" arXiv: 1706.03762, 2017.\n\n[4] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv: 1512.03385, 2015.\n\n[5] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie, et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv: 2501.01234, 2025.\nPAPER_EOF"
}'}

